ald's  so which one any guesses here  i guess there's actually a mcdonald's in  the top image so i hope everyone's going  to say that  but essentially we want to see whether  this task is feasible at all to begin  with because it's such a weird you know  you wouldn't imagine that people would  be able to do this uh you know  statistically significantly  so and as well as we want to see if  machines can do this right  so we put man versus machine so  sim you know we use our favorite  crowdsourcing interface  amazon mechanical turk where you can  just query a bunch of people about  simple tasks or complex tasks and pay  them accordingly  so we uh ask them  you know we show them two images and we  just ask them which one do you think is  closer to mcdonald's  so if they were acting at chance we  would get an  accuracy of about 50 percent  and similarly we can ask the machine we  give it an image  we convert it into some sort of  representation that the computer  understands  we apply our favorite machine learning  algorithm and  use that to predict uh what is the  distance to mcdonald's  so what happens when we tried this so  fortunately  it did not you know people were doing  well above chance at about 60 percent  so about 60 of pairs of images they were  able to correctly tell that oh look this  one is probably closer to mcdonald's  than the other one  which was kind of nice so we're like  good machines do the same thing  and fortunately or you know surprisingly  actually  machines performed even better than  humans  at doing this task and then we tried it  out in a bunch of other cities so  this was again using you know really big  data it was  about i think 20 million images that we  sampled from google street view  and we applied it to like a bunch of  them  here so similarly we see that in most of  the cities  machines are actually able to outperform  humans in  understanding what's going on in this  large  data set to you know figure out which  images are closer or farther from  mcdonald's  so this was just a simple task where you  do a binary decision  but now can we actually find our way to  mcdonald's we really want that happy  meal so  imagine i drop you at some random  location in manhattan  and i'm like go try and find mcdonald's  based on  uh you know the visual cues you get from  the environment don't talk to people  or use your phone so can you do this  navigation task  uh where you just you know step on these  grids that's how we set it up for the  machine  uh to find your way to mcdonald's  and surprisingly people are actually  able to do pretty well  you know compared to just doing like a  random walk of the environment people  can get there  many many times faster and there's  another part of the result  which shows that the random walk  actually fails you know most of the time  while people are fairly good at  navigating to  you know within like 50 meters off a  mcdonald's uh  as well as computers which do a  reasonable job  so we have a we'll soon have a demo  online at you know mcdonalds.co.mit.edu  so you guys can  try it out and see if you can find your  way to mcdonald's  so this was the first step you know just  looking right outside the image  or looking at the environment uh around  the image  so the next one is let's try and predict  how many people are going to like a  particular image  so what exactly is image popularity so  different networks have different  notions of this uh for example on  instagram it might be the number of  people that like a particular image  on facebook it might similarly be be the  number of people that like it  or the number of comments the image gets  so  uh on you know something like twitter it  might again be the number of retweets  favorites and so on  but for this study we used a flicker  which actually reports the number of  views  on a particular image which to me is  like a more pure metric  because there are many times you might  open an image  but not necessarily click like on it as  an example if your friend uploads an  image like that you might be  you know you might take a look at it but  you might not want to press like  out of you know feeling  yeah for obvious reasons or for example  there's a funeral scene it might be you  know you might open it take a look but  pressing like may be  not very uh nice essentially so  whereas views actually gives you all of  this so as long as i've clicked on the  image  it registers a view so it actually tells  you like it gives you a more pure metric  of what people  actually look at so why exactly  does it matter why do we want to predict  uh which images are going to be popular  so firstly you know we want to do  content distribution  across our network so if we can predict  that this image is likely to get  extremely high traffic  we can prepare for that secondly i think  this is one of the biggest problems  uh you know plaguing teens uh  of this generation is the problem of  selfie selection  uh people take so many selfies and they  don't know which one to upload  imagine if i could tell you look this  selfie is probably going to get you more  likes than this other one  won't it just save them a bunch of time  instead of spending hours  you know doing this they could be  studying or so  another obvious uh application of this  is also in advertising  um we would you know like you want to  select images that are likely to get  popular  you want virality you want to figure  this out before you put your  ad campaign online so just as an example  this is one of  an advert for funeral services  so there's actually a bunch of other uh  applications of this like you know video  summarization  you want to understand user behavior and  so on  so how did we go about doing this  first we generate a data set of 2.3 like  million flickr images where we just  download  a whole bunch of random flickr images we  get the views on these images  and we use our again our same pipeline  that we had before  to try and predict how many views the  image is going to get  so just using the content and ignoring a  lot of the social features which tend to  have  a big impact uh on these metrics such as  you know how many friends you have  like what groups you're part of and so  on  we just used the content of the image  which is something that people had not  looked at before so we want to try and  figure out you know using different  types of  image features which ones  are going to have a bigger impact so for  example these are just some popular  vision features like texture gradient  and so on and deep learning features  which have recently become quite popular  for those of you who may have heard of  it  as well as some high level features such  as the type of objects in the image  so surprisingly we can actually do  pretty well  uh predicting you know at a rank  correlation of 0.4  uh which images are going to be more  popular but i think the more interesting  part is looking at what causes the  popularity  so for example we can start looking at  which colors  tend to increase the popularity of  images so we tend to find like you know  more bright colors more  greens sorry more reds and so tend to  have a bigger impact as compared to  uh like the more greens and blues which  might suggest that you know indoor  images tend to be slightly more  like tend to have a higher popularity uh  as compared to outdoor seat type images  so this kind of the stuff people like  looking at  uh the next thing is let's try and  predict what like let's try and figure  out what objects tend to cause  you know more or less popular images so  we use a similar  uh pipeline as before where we now  instead of having you know these complex  image features we have  uh the object likelihood which just says  these are the objects present in the  image  now use this to predict uh what's going  like you know which ones are going to be  popular  and why we have to do this is because  it's  you know my advisor was like you can  either finish your phd  or we can use that money to label the  2.3 million images we have so  you know which one i chose and instead  we applied the best like computer vision  algorithm we know  uh to try and predict the objects  instead of getting them labeled  uh by hand so these are some of the  results from that  um we find that you know some of the  rare objects such as i guess  giant like having giant pandas or  ladybugs uh  you know if you can find these they tend  to have a medium positive impact  uh on popularity but the objects that  have the most positive impact  uh are these ones so  you know it's obvious why guys like them  and i guess girls like checking out  other girls  so uh it's like universally acceptable  like  or universally popular uh and then the  objects that have negative impact  uh tend to be like spatulas so you know  stop having spatulas in your images  or plungers or the photos that everyone  loves taking  probably possibly in this room is with  your laptop stop doing that  it does not help the popularity of your  images so  this thing was actually uh pretty fun so  we decided to make a demo where people  could upload  uh their images online to check this  thing so  within like i think so you know it was  on a bunch of like media outlets and  such  uh within i think three days we had  about 300 000 images  uploaded uh which was awesome and you  know we had a lot of content  but it was amazing to see how passionate  people were  about this uh this is one of my favorite  emails from the time  she had uploaded some images and she was  like these girls you know they deserve a  better score and  she was extremely like into uh  the calculator and it was just nice to  see uh  you know compared to most of the other  research that goes on  how much people care about this stuff  uh so the next step is so one you know  first we were predicting popularity now  we want so that was predicting what  people like but now we want to try and  predict what people remember  from images so what exactly is  memorability so  let me just define vulnerability as the  likelihood of remembering a particular  image  this is kind of boring so why don't you  guy so i think the best way for you guys  to learn about this is to participate  in the experiment itself so we will play  a visual memory game  uh you guys are just going to clap your  hands anytime you see an image that you  have seen before  so images will flash one image per  second and  if you've seen them like if you see the  image in this sequence before just clap  your hands alright  everybody got it let me see those hands  okay  they're not supposed to clap on it  okay this usually works better uh but  yeah  all right so you guys were clapping so  this experiment was conducted on amazon  mechanical turk  uh where people were seeing these  repeats and pressing a key whenever they  saw one  and memorability can now be defined  as the probability of correctly  detecting this repeat so the  in this case i'd put the repeats you  know maybe within like five images after  uh but on mturk these repeats were about  100 images after so there are many times  that  images were forgotten but a large  proportion of the times the images were  remembered  so the really interesting aspect of this  uh  is if i now you know suppose i have 100  people participate in my experiment  and i divide them into two groups  of like 50 people each and plot the  memorability or you know this  probability of correctly detecting a  repeat  for each of these two groups  independently i get a graph something  like that  so you just sort it by the memorability  and the very interesting thing is  they actually correspond to each other  so despite you know having completely  random workers on mturk who might be  i don't know like housewives in the u.s  like  rickshaw drivers in india and you know  people who are just participating from  around the world  despite having varied completely varied  backgrounds  their the extent to which they remember  different images is actually the same  so you know you and i the probability  with which we'll remember  a given image is actually a property of  the image and not of the individual  so which is really cool and as we added  more and more people  into the experiment you know that's the  number of scores per image means  each image was viewed by like 80 or so  people  uh the rank correlation between the two  groups kept increasing  and there was a point where we kind of  ran out of money so  and you know you can split these two  groups in any way you want and  you get a really high rank correlation  every single time  which suggests that memorability is an  intrinsic  and measurable property of an image and  just another interesting aspect is  look at the range of differences so  there's a lot of images that you know  less than like 40 of people remember  while there are some images that more  than 90  remember so there's just some images  that you know tend to stick in our minds  while others  are completely forgotten and  this means that we can actually start  you know playing with  the content of the image to affect how  much we will remember it  and starting to make a lot of low  memorability images  memorable so but before we can do that  we need to you know be modifying  memorability in a predictable way  so first we need to be able to predict  memorability  so first we just tried some simple  statistics right like  maybe having a really bright image makes  it extremely memorable or just having a  lot of red in the image makes it  memorable  but what we found is that there was not  very much correlation with any of these  simple  statistics uh and what people remember  so the next thing we tried was you know  the standard computer vision features  and we're like oh this is pretty good  uh we're getting you know somewhere we  can actually predict with  a rank correlation of 0.5 while humans  only have a consistency of 0.75 so we're  you know two-thirds of the way there  so that's pretty good now we're like  you know in computer vision and many  other uh  like fields like machine learning what  we have is ground truth  is what people say so we thought  why don't we ask people what do they  think they will remember so we have this  way you know we have our study we have  uh some approach of measuring what they  actually remembered  and then the other question we can ask  is what do you think you will remember  and we had to create a special place up  top for how people  performed they actually behaved as good  as random number generators so  people have no idea what kind of images  they're going to remember they just  like it's yeah we tried the question in  many different ways and this is the best  uh kind of we could do most people just  people are just guessing which is  actually kind of great  because uh that means that  it has a lot of room for algorithms  which can predict memorability  to you know make a difference here  because if i ask you to  be if i give you an image and be like  all right use photoshop or do whatever  you want  and try and make this image more  memorable you'd have no idea how to do  it because  you don't even know what the notion of  memorability is  and you don't even know how memorable  this image is versus what happens after  you make changes  while the algorithms can actually do a  reasonable job at predicting this  so there's a like it's a you know very  diff like it's a machine learning task  turned on its head  uh where humans are terrible at it  so that brings us to modifying  memorability  so imagine if something like this was  possible imagine if i could change  the extent to which you remember images  how could this be  useful so firstly obviously in  advertising right like imagine if i just  had to show you an image once you  remember it sticks in your mind  you know i've cluttered your mind with  garbage but  you just can't forget this image the  other one is logos right  people keep advertising and putting  logos everywhere just in hopes that  next time you see this logo you have  some brand familiarity  now imagine if i can change your logo to  just make it  extremely memorable so you show it once  people remember it  so these are some like i guess people  would call it like adverse uh  applications but think about you know  good ones now if in education uh where  we have a lot of  content that we need to memorize if we  just changed  visually how it's presented uh we could  make the task of  remembering all this so much easier  you know imagine if you had a harder  time like forgetting it than remembering  it  uh another obvious one mobile  applications we just want to  you know change all our images to be  more memorable imagine a new memorable  filter on instagram  uh our social networking where we're  posting uh  all the things we do and we want our  friends to remember it  as well as face memorability right like  you know  imagine like a job application where out  of whatever some number of resumes  your face is the one that's remembered  so  in this current uh work i looked at  phase modifying face memorability  specifically  so given a face like this uh can we  actually  you know move it along this memorability  axis while still trying to preserve the  identity of this guy we don't want to  lose  him but we want to change the image  subtly  to make it more or less memorable so  these are just some examples  uh produced so you can see you know some  of the lighting has changed uh  there's there's some minor effects that  happen you know some like slightly the  eyes  the corners have changed but it actually  has a pretty big  impact on how much we remember the  different images so over here you know  putting them side by side you guys can  see the differences  but if i you know showed you one then  showed you the other you'd be like  that's the same image i just saw  uh which seems to suggest that you know  the identity is preserved and so on so  how do we go about doing this  uh let's just we have some space of a  face let's say  x where the face is parameterized by  this x  and we can move it in this space so just  imagine you know  you have those points you can just hold  and drag  right like you can drag it in different  dimensions and such so you can drag like  you drag it in one way that's like you  know the guy's face becomes fatter  another way it becomes thinner he looks  younger and so on so  you now have the space where you can  change you can start modifying a face  so we now define a cost function to  you know how do we want to change this  face  so the first thing we want to do is we  want to preserve identity so  if we change identity the cost function  goes up  secondly we want to preserve some  attributes so you know usu  hopefully by the end of the modification  the dude doesn't start looking like a  girl or some you know  or vice versa uh and the last so you  know these two will keep the face  exactly the same so now we need a  competing term which will  cause it to have a predictable effect on  memorability  which is again you know which is the the  last term so this term is going to say  you know the current memorability is x  we want it to be x plus 0.5  for example and now try and modify the  face  under these constraints so you know i  won't go more into like the math and  such  but uh here are just some example  results  so faces you know i think one of like  these are just three results but one of  the things that you can see that's  happening for  more memorable faces is they tend to get  like slightly thinner  the ship the shape of the lips and such  changes a little bit  uh as well as just the expression of the  person  has also changed and a lot of these  things are extremely hard to notice like  if i gave you  i don't know i'll give you 10 000 images  with memorability scores now go and  figure out  uh you know what exactly causes it it's  extremely hard to determine this by  looking  just staring at that data whereas for  the machine it's  much easier to parse through all that  data and identify what are some of these  characteristics affecting  memorability and it's actually able to  do it in a fairly predictable way so  out of all the modifications we made 75  percent of them  were completely predictable so the graph  you know the blue one is showing the  ones where we took a face  and increased its memorability and the  red one is where we took the same face  and decreased its memorability  and it's you know it's happening uh  pretty consistently  so i guess the end goal is you know  something like this where we have a  iphone app where people are just able to  you know  twiddle their memorability around uh as  well as  change so there's some other attributes  that we've been working on as well like  you want to try looking more confident  you want to look more intelligent you  want to look  you know all these soft attributes apart  from the ones that are  i don't know if you've seen on the app  store there's a lot of apps for looking  just  more attractive and that's all you know  putting makeup and such but  imagine a whole bunch of other  applications where you want to put this  photo on your cv you don't want to just  necessarily look more attractive but you  want to be maybe more confident  more intelligent and so on and you can  start  hopefully doing this someday so yeah a  lot of this work was covered again by  you know popular media outlets and such  uh which was great  we got a lot of feedback and so on  so i guess just to reiterate what i had  covered first we started  you know our goal was to look beyond the  visible scene and we started by looking  what is next to the image finding  mcdonald's and then predicting  how people would react to a particular  image which is whether they're going to  like it in terms of popularity  or whether they will remember it and  lastly how can we modify some of these  non-visual properties by changing the  visual elements of the image  which was the modifying vulnerability so  i just want to thank my co-authors and  funding sources and such  and obviously thanks to all you guys for  being here
 

